Objectives

identify the unit of analysis and grain;
distinguish observations, variables, values, and identifiers;
evaluate whether a table follows tidy-data principles;
distinguish dimensions from measures; and
classify variables using appropriate measurement scales.

7) Identify common analysis mistakes
For each mistake, explain how you would detect or prevent it:

wrong unit of analysis;
wrong data structure;
wrong variable type;
incorrect aggregation; and
assuming a field means something without checking its documentation.

Objectives

match common analytical questions to appropriate chart types;
distinguish comparison, composition, distribution, time, and relationship views;
evaluate visual encodings, ordering, color, labels, and clutter;
identify misleading or unnecessary design choices; and
justify a visualization for a specific audience and purpose.

5) Justify one choice
Choose a question from a current ECO 230 dataset. In 4-6 sentences:

state the question and intended audience;
name the variables and their measurement types;
select a chart and explain why its visual encoding fits the comparison;
identify 2 design decisions you would apply; and
name one limitation the visualization would not resolve.

Objectives

explain why sample estimates require an uncertainty check;
interpret p-values, confidence intervals, and Bayes factors correctly;
state null and alternative hypotheses in context;
match an analytical story and its variables to an appropriate test; and
separate statistical evidence from practical importance.

Objectives

distinguish populations, sampling frames, samples, and response pools;
compare probability and nonprobability sampling methods;
distinguish randomness, representativeness, sampling error, and bias;
identify common sources of survey error;
evaluate question wording and response options; and
state conclusions that fit the sampling and data-collection process.

Data literacy is the ability to read, work with, analyze, and communicate with data to inform decisions. It includes understanding what data represent, choosing appropriate methods, recognizing limitations, and explaining results to an audience.
In ECO 230, data literacy includes the ability to:

identify the source, structure, and unit of analysis of a dataset;
distinguish variable types and levels of measurement;
choose appropriate descriptive summaries and introductory inferential methods;
select and create effective, ethical data visualizations;
interpret statistical output in context; and
communicate evidence clearly in writing, visuals, and presentations.

